+# Decision: Preview cache eviction policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private 12 GiB cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets. The archive holds 90,000 clips with about 6,000 distinct clips viewed each week, so the cache cannot hold everything and must choose what to evict on admission. The access pattern is mixed and the mix varies by production: editors alternate long sequential screenings — which stream many sheets viewed once — with comparison sessions that repeatedly revisit a small working set. The eviction policy decides, under that mixed load, whether the reused working set survives a long screening or is flushed by it. Getting it wrong degrades the comparison workflow precisely when an editor is leaning on it.
+
+Every candidate keeps its bookkeeping private to the worker and leaves the preview interface, render-queue concurrency, and 12 GiB budget unchanged; those are standing invariants, not differentiators here.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates a candidate — all three satisfy the standing invariants above. Selection turns on four weighted criteria.
+
+- **Scan resistance (major).** Whether a long sequential screening of one-use sheets can evict the reused comparison working set. This is the dominant driver: the HLD's problem statement names the screening/comparison alternation as the workload the cache exists to serve.
+- **Adaptation on project switch (moderate).** How quickly the resident set follows a change of working set when an editor moves to a different project, rather than holding yesterday's sheets.
+- **Bookkeeping cost (moderate).** Per-request and per-entry work, and the number of structures the worker must maintain.
+- **Tuning burden (minor).** Workload-specific parameters that must be set and retuned to get good behaviour.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description.** A single recency-ordered queue. Every hit moves an entry to the most-recently-used end; admission evicts the least-recently-used entry until the incoming sheet fits the 12 GiB budget. No per-entry counters or secondary structures.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scan resistance: **weak.** A screening longer than the cache touches each sheet once and walks the whole queue, evicting the comparison working set as it goes.
+- Adaptation on project switch: **strong.** Recency follows the new working set immediately; stale sheets fall to the tail and leave.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong.** One queue, one move-to-front per hit, no counters.
+- Tuning burden: **strong.** No parameters.
+
+**Summary.** Cheapest and quickest to adapt, but a long screening evicts the sheets comparison depends on.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description.** Each entry carries an access counter; eviction removes the lowest-count entries. Counts are periodically aged (decayed) so that once-popular sheets lose standing over time rather than pinning the cache forever.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scan resistance: **partial.** One-use scan sheets accumulate low counts and are evicted first, protecting popular clips — but a reused sheet is protected only after its count has risen, and the aging rate governs how long protection lasts.
+- Adaptation on project switch: **weak.** Yesterday's favourites retain high counts until aging decays them, so the policy lags a switch to a new project's working set.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak.** A counter per entry plus a periodic aging pass over the cache.
+- Tuning burden: **moderate.** The aging rate must be chosen and retuned; too slow pins stale clips, too fast collapses toward recency.
+
+**Summary.** Best at retaining repeatedly popular clips, at the cost of counter maintenance and a lag when the working set changes.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** Two resident queues plus a ghost list. **A1in** is a FIFO of sheets seen once since admission; **Am** is an LRU of sheets that have been reused; **A1out** is a FIFO of recently evicted keys only, holding no bytes. A first sighting enters A1in. Reclaiming space evicts A1in's tail first, recording its key in A1out; only when A1in is within its byte sub-budget does eviction fall to Am's LRU tail. A request for a key found in A1out is admitted to Am — reuse of a key the cache recently held. A hit on a sheet still resident in A1in does not promote it. A tunable byte split sizes A1in against Am, and A1out is capped by key count.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scan resistance: **strong.** A one-pass screening fills A1in and drains out through A1out without ever entering Am, so the reused working set in Am is untouched by the scan.
+- Adaptation on project switch: **partial.** Am is LRU, so a new project's reused sheets displace the old working set as they are re-referenced — quicker than aged counts, slower than plain LRU because displacement only happens through the A1out promotion path.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **moderate.** Two resident queues and a key-only ghost list; no per-entry counters and no periodic pass, but admission must consult A1out and route between queues.
+- Tuning burden: **minor.** The A1in/Am byte split and the A1out cap must be set, but they are structural sizes, not a continuously-sensitive decay rate.
+
+**Summary.** Protects the reused working set from one-off scans, at the cost of a second queue, a ghost list, and a size split to tune; a sheet re-touched *before* it is evicted is not yet counted as reused.
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q is chosen.** The major criterion is scan resistance, and it is the one criterion on which the options genuinely diverge: LRU is weak on it and frequency-with-aging only partial, while 2Q is strong by construction — a long screening cannot reach Am. 2Q concedes ground on the moderate criteria (its bookkeeping is heavier than LRU's, and it adapts to a project switch more slowly than LRU though faster than aged counts), and these are accepted to secure the comparison working set during big screenings.
+
+Committing to 2Q forecloses the single-queue simplicity of LRU and obligates two things downstream: the A1in/Am byte split and the A1out cap must be tuned against per-production workloads (initial values are the literature's starting ratios, not final), and the policy carries one accepted weak spot — a sheet re-viewed before it is evicted from A1in is not promoted to Am until it leaves and returns, so a tight double-touch within a screening is not protected.
+
+This decision is not backed by a named HLD tenet; it turns directly on the problem statement's screening/comparison workload. The absence of a tenet for "protect the reused working set" is a candidate gap in the HLD worth raising separately.
